<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Juan de Garay</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1528">1528</a>–<a href="/years/1583">1583</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Juan de Garay was a Basque-born conquistador who served in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a>. From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asuncion">Asunción</a> he founded Santa Fe in 1573 and refounded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> in 1580, decades after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-mendoza">Pedro de Mendoza</a>'s first settlement had been abandoned. As governor in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a> he distributed land and native labour, and he was killed by indigenous warriors in 1583.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/juan-de-garay.jpg" alt="Juan de Garay" width="318" height="454" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Garay.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Garay grew up in the household of his uncle Pedro Ortiz de Zárate, whose family had some standing: the uncle was appointed judge and alcalde mayor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segovia">Segovia</a>, and a cousin attended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-salamanca">University of Salamanca</a>. He was also related to Juan Ortiz de Zárate, third adelantado of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a>. In 1543 he sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> with his uncle in the first expedition of Viceroy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blasco-nunez-vela">Blasco Núñez Vela</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1561 Garay took part in founding Santa Cruz de la Sierra, and in 1568 he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asuncion">Asunción</a>, where he rose to political prominence. In April 1573 the governor of Asunción sent him with eighty men on an expedition to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parana-river">Paraná River</a>, during which he founded Santa Fe de la Vera Cruz. Appointed governor of Asunción in 1576, he set out to establish Indian villages and systems of local government among the indigenous peoples.</p><p>Having reached the rank of captain general, he refounded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> on 11 June 1580. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-mendoza">Pedro de Mendoza</a>'s settlement of 1536, Nuestra Señora del Buen Ayre, had been destroyed by the natives. Garay landed at the place of the present Plaza de Mayo and named the city Ciudad de la <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a> and its port Santa Maria de Buenos Ayres. In 1581–1582 he led an expedition in search of the City of the Caesars.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 20 March 1583, travelling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> to Santa Fe with 40 men, a Franciscan priest and a few women, Garay camped on the banks of the Carcarañá River near the old Sancti Spíritus Fort. Querandíes warriors ambushed the group and killed Garay, the priest, a woman and twelve of the soldiers.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> became the main city of the Paraná basin and its most important port. Garay's daughter Jerónima de Contreras married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernandarias">Hernando Arias de Saavedra</a>, governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a>, and a house he built, the palace-baserri Garatikua, stands in the oldest part of the town of Garay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biscay">Biscay</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Juan de Garay” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hunger and attacks forced its abandonment in 1541, and <b>Juan de Garay</b> refounded Buenos Aires in 1580.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-2">2 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1617 the Governorate of the Río de la Plata was divided, and the southern part became the Governorate of Buenos Aires, with its capital at Buenos Aires, refounded by <b>Juan de Garay</b> in 1580.</blockquote><a href="/state/governorate-of-buenos-aires">Governorate of Buenos Aires</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Juan de Garay</b> founded Santa Fe in 1573 and refounded Buenos Aires in 1580.</blockquote><a href="/state/governorate-of-the-rio-de-la-plata">Governorate of the Río de la Plata</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Orduña-Urduña, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43,-3.01&amp;year=1563" aria-label="Orduña-Urduña on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Juan de Garay"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4812 3896h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4812 3896h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43,-3.01&amp;year=1563">What was here in 1563? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
